        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES: <br>
Research on the International Space Station provides a unique opportunity to study the effects of microgravity on the central nervous system.  Ground research in 1-g makes it difficult to differentiate the various sensorimotor responses to visual cues.  By performing tasks in microgravity, measurements of sensory information can be obtained while manipulating visual and tactile cues.
<p>
The objective of this study is to test a single subject to assess the hardware and procedures.  This will allow for fine-tuning the procedures and parameters to maximize the science return for the future crew participating in the experiment.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Test subjects will be using a three dimensional (3D) virtual reality system to approximate the task of shooting a projectile through target openings.  Visual and tactile cues will vary during each trial.  Training will be provided on the use of the 3D tracking system and on the tasking to be performed.  Data will be acquired preflight, postflight, and on-orbit.  
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
This is an international experiment. NASA does not currently have an agreement with international space partners to archive their data in the LSDA.
<p>
        </div>

